.TH CTPOOL_WAIT 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_wait \- block until the pool is idle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_wait(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks until the task queue is empty and every active task has stopped.
It does not shut the pool down: every worker thread stays alive, and the
caller can submit new tasks after this call returns.

Contract: no other thread should submit a task while ctpool_wait runs,
because with an unbounded queue a submission from another thread can
block this call for ever.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ctpool_wait waits for the queue and for the tasks that workers run, but
not for the on_complete callbacks of discarded tasks. A
ctpool_shutdown_immediate(3) on another thread empties the queue at
once and then calls the on_complete of each discarded task, with
ran == false, on its own thread, so ctpool_wait can return while those
callbacks still run. To know that every one of them has finished, wait
for the ctpool_shutdown_immediate call itself to return.

By default the library treats a pool that a child inherited across a
fork(2) call as idle, because fork(2) does not duplicate the worker
threads of that pool into the child. ctpool_wait on such a pool in the
child therefore returns at once instead of waiting for progress that no
worker thread in that process can ever make.

A call to ctpool_wait on pool from inside a task that runs on one of the
worker threads of pool, or from the on_complete callback of that task,
is also idle by default and returns at once. The pool still counts the
calling task among its own active tasks until that task returns, so
without this rule a wait here would deadlock the calling worker against
itself.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
